
=== Part 1: Development with morloc-manager

This is the first of two tutorials covering the morloc-manager workflow.
Part 1 covers installation, environment management, and developing morloc
programs. link:tutorial-deployment.asc[Part 2] covers serving, freezing,
and deploying.

==== Why morloc-manager

Morloc programs combine functions from multiple languages -- Python, R,
{cpp} -- under one type system. The compiler, language runtimes, and
standard library modules all need to coexist in a consistent environment.
`morloc-manager` provides that environment as a named, isolated container
with its own module store, compiler version, and optional system
dependencies.

You work on the host, edit files in your normal editor, and run
`morloc-manager run` to execute commands inside the container. Your
working directory is bind-mounted automatically, so there is no file
copying.

==== Step 0: Prerequisites

Download the `morloc-manager` binary from the
https://github.com/morloc-project/morloc/releases[GitHub releases page].
Binaries are published for x86 Linux, Linux ARM, and macOS. On macOS you
may need to clear the quarantine attribute:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ xattr -d com.apple.quarantine morloc-manager
----

You also need a container engine: Podman (recommended for rootless local
work) or Docker.

If both engines are installed, pick a default:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager setup --engine podman
Engine set to: podman
----

If only one engine is installed, `new` auto-detects it and no setup step
is required. You can check the current configuration at any time:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager setup
----

==== Step 1: Create an environment

An *environment* is a named, self-contained Morloc installation: a
container image, a data directory for modules and binaries, and optionally
a custom Dockerfile layer with extra dependencies. Everything in this
tutorial happens inside one.

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager new dnd --version 0.79.3
Pulling ghcr.io/morloc-project/morloc/morloc-full:0.79.3...
Created environment: dnd
Initializing morloc (this may take several minutes)...
Environment 'dnd' is ready.
Activate it with: morloc-manager select dnd
----

Here I'm using v0.79.3, which is the latest version at the time of
writing. Future versions may deviate from this tutorial since backwards
compatibility won't be guaranteed until v1.0. For the latest documentation,
see https://morloc-project.github.io/docs.

NOTE: `morloc-manager new` runs `morloc init` inside the container as part
of setup, which compiles the shared library, the nexus binary, and the
language bindings. You will see compilation output from gcc/g++, Python
`build_ext`, and possibly a warning like `Skipping Julia setup` -- this is
expected; the environment is still ready.

If you omit `--version`, morloc-manager pulls the `:edge` image (latest
development build) and records the resolved version. You can also run the
interactive wizard by calling `morloc-manager new` with no arguments -- it
prompts for name and base image.

Select the environment so that subsequent commands target it:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager select dnd
Selected environment: dnd
----

Verify it:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager info
Active:         dnd
Local engine:   podman
System engine:  unset
SELinux:        not detected

Directories:
  Config (local)       ~/.config/morloc (exists)
  Data (local)         ~/.local/share/morloc (exists)
  Config (system)      /etc/morloc (not found)
  Data (system)        /usr/local/share/morloc (not found)

Local environments:
  dnd [0.79.3] (active)
----

More detailed information is available with `morloc-manager info dnd`:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager info dnd
Name:           dnd
Scope:          local
Active:         yes
Base image:     ghcr.io/morloc-project/morloc/morloc-full:0.79.3
Morloc version: 0.79.3
Engine:         podman
SHM size:       512m
Dockerfile:     none
Flags:          /home/weena/.config/morloc/environments/dnd/env.flags
Data dir:       /home/weena/.local/share/morloc/environments/dnd
----

==== Step 2: Install modules and write a program

`morloc-manager run` executes a command inside the active environment's
container. Your current working directory is bind-mounted into the
container, so files you create on the host are visible inside, and vice
versa.

Use `--` to separate `morloc-manager` flags from inner command flags.
Without `--`, flags like `-o` or `--version` are consumed by
`morloc-manager run` itself rather than passed to the inner command.

Morloc's standard library is split into modules hosted on GitHub. Install
the ones this tutorial needs:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager run -- morloc install root root-py
----

Each `morloc install` fetches a module and places it in the environment's
`lib/` directory. `root` provides core type definitions; `root-py`
provides Python implementations of those types.

Create a new project directory, `dnd/`, and write the Morloc module:

[source, morloc]
.dnd.loc
----
module dnd (maximum, roll, rollAdv)

import root-py

source Py from "dice.py" ("roll")

--' Roll N dice with K pips per die
roll ::
  --' Number of dice to roll
  --' metavar: N
  Int ->
  --' Number of pips on each die
  --' metavar: K
  Int ->
  --' List of N dice roll results
  <Rand> [Int]

--' Get the maximum value in a list of integers
maximum :: [Int] -> Int
maximum = fold1 max

--' Roll 2d20 and take the higher result
rollAdv :: <Rand> Int
rollAdv = do
  xs <- roll 2 20
  maximum xs
----

The `<Rand>` tag in the return type is an effect annotation marking this
function as non-deterministic. It tells the Morloc type system that calling
`roll` may return different results each time.

And the Python implementation:

[source, python]
.dice.py
----
import random

def roll(n, d):
    return [random.randint(1, d) for _ in range(n)]
----

Build and run. The `--shell` flag drops you into an interactive shell
inside the container:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager run --shell
container$ morloc make dnd.loc
container$ ./dnd rollAdv
18
container$ ./dnd rollAdv
7
container$ exit
----

Or build and test in a single command:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager run -- bash -c "morloc make dnd.loc && ./dnd rollAdv"
18
----

The compiled program is a self-contained CLI: each exported non-generic
term becomes a subcommand.

==== Step 3: Add cross-language functions

So far the program is pure Python. One of Morloc's distinctive features
is that you can swap a function's implementation to another language
without changing the composition. Let's implement `roll` in R and `max`
in {cpp}.

[source, r]
.dice.R
----
roll <- function(n, d) {
    sample(1:d, n, replace = TRUE)
}
----

[source, c++]
.dice.hpp
----
#pragma once
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

template <typename A>
A max(const std::vector<A>& xs) {
    return *std::max_element(xs.begin(), xs.end());
}
----

Update `dice.py` with a narration function:

[source, python]
.dice.py
----
import random

def roll(n, d):
    return [random.randint(1, d) for _ in range(n)]

def narrate(n):
    return f"You rolled a {n}"
----

Update `dnd.loc` to source from three languages:

[source, morloc]
.dnd.loc
----
module dnd (rollAdv)

import root-py
import root-cpp
import root-r

source R from "dice.R" ("roll")
source Cpp from "dice.hpp" ("max")
source Py from "dice.py" ("narrate")

roll :: Int -> Int -> <Rand> [Int]
max :: [Int] -> Int
narrate :: Int -> Str

rollAdv = do
  rolls <- roll 2 20
  narrate (max rolls)
----

Install the language-specific root modules:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager run -- morloc install root-r root-cpp
----

Build and test:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager run -- bash -c "morloc make dnd.loc && ./dnd rollAdv"
"You rolled a 19"
----

The composition is unchanged. Morloc compiled a {cpp} pool, a Python
pool, and an R pool, and the nexus routes each call to the correct
runtime with automatic serialization between them.

==== Step 4: Package metadata

Every Morloc project needs a `package.yaml` that describes the package and
lists which files to bundle when installing. Initialize one with
`morloc new`:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager run -- morloc new dnd
Created package.yaml for 'dnd'
----

NOTE: `morloc new` (inside the container) initializes a Morloc package --
it creates `package.yaml`. This is different from `morloc-manager new`
(on the host), which creates an environment.

By default, `morloc make --install` bundles all files in the project
directory (filtered by `.morlocignore`). For simple projects like this one,
that is sufficient -- you do not need to edit `package.yaml`.

For larger projects, you can explicitly list source files in the `include`
field to control what gets bundled:

[source, yaml]
.package.yaml
----
name: dnd
version: 0.1.0
synopsis: A dice roller demo
license: MIT
dependencies: []
include:
  - "dice.py"
  - "dice.R"
  - "dice.hpp"
----

The `include` field accepts exact file paths (`"dice.py"`), directories
with a trailing slash (`"src/"`), and glob patterns (`"*.py"`,
`"**/*.R"`). When `include` is specified:

* Every file you reference with `source ... from "path"` in a `.loc` file
  must be covered by at least one `include` entry. `morloc make --install`
  errors out if a directly sourced file is missing from `include`.
  Transitive dependencies (e.g., one R file calling `source()` on another)
  are _not_ auto-discovered, so you must list those yourself.
* All `include` paths are resolved relative to the directory that holds
  `package.yaml`. Absolute paths and `..` traversals are rejected --
  installs must stay self-contained within the project directory. The
  relative subdirectory layout is preserved on install (a file listed as
  `src/helpers/util.py` lands at `.../src/helpers/util.py`, not
  `.../util.py`).

==== Step 5: Add a system dependency

Real projects often need system packages beyond what the base Morloc image
provides. You can layer a custom Dockerfile on top of the base image
without leaving the `morloc-manager` interface.

Generate a stub Dockerfile:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager update dnd --dockerfile-stub
Updating environment: dnd
Stub Dockerfile: ~/.config/morloc/environments/dnd/Dockerfile
Edit it, then run: morloc-manager update dnd
Environment 'dnd' updated.
----

Edit that file to install `jq` (a common JSON processing tool):

[source, dockerfile]
.~/.config/morloc/environments/dnd/Dockerfile
----
ARG CONTAINER_BASE=scratch
FROM ${CONTAINER_BASE}

RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y jq && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*
----

The `CONTAINER_BASE` build arg is substituted with the environment's base
image at build time. Rebuild:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager update dnd
Updating environment: dnd
...
Environment 'dnd' updated.
----

Verify the new tool is available inside the container:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager run -- jq --version
jq-1.7.1
----

This same mechanism works for any system package, Python library, R
package, or arbitrary Dockerfile instruction. A content hash on the
Dockerfile lets `update` skip the rebuild when nothing has changed.

You can also include extra files in the build context using the `-i` flag:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager update dnd --dockerfile ./Dockerfile -i ./requirements.txt
----

The `jq` package is just a stand-in here -- the dice roller doesn't need
it. In a real project you would add whatever your code actually depends on
(e.g., `libcurl-dev`, a Python package via `pip install`, or an R package
via `install.packages()`).

==== Step 6: Managing environments

As you work on more projects, you'll accumulate environments. Here is how
to manage them.

List all environments:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager ls
Local environments:
  dnd [0.79.3] (active)
----

Switch between environments:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager new experiments --version 0.79.3
$ morloc-manager select experiments
Selected environment: experiments
$ morloc-manager select dnd
Selected environment: dnd
----

Show detailed info:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager info dnd
----

Update an existing environment. The `update` command accepts the same
flags as `new` but defaults to keeping existing values:

[%nowrap, console]
----
# Rebuild after editing the Dockerfile
$ morloc-manager update dnd

# Change shared memory size
$ morloc-manager update dnd --shm-size 1g

# Upgrade to a new morloc version and re-initialize
$ morloc-manager update dnd --version 0.80.0 --reinit
----

Remove an environment you no longer need:

[%nowrap, console]
----
$ morloc-manager rm experiments
Removed environment: experiments
----

==== What's next

At this point you can create environments, install modules, write
cross-language morloc programs, add system dependencies, and manage
multiple environments.

link:tutorial-deployment.asc[Part 2] picks up from here and covers
installing programs permanently, serving them over HTTP, exporting
portable images, system-wide environments, and health checks with
`doctor`.
